## 1. Introduction
In the context of carbon neutrality, solar power has been universally recognized as one of the most promising renewable energy sources and is profitable in terms of conserving energy and reducing carbon emissions [1]. A rapidly growing number of experts contend that photocatalytic water splitting to evolve hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ has been proposed as a prospective approach that can ameliorate the global energy crisis [2]. Based on the photocatalytic mechanism that involves the excitation, separation, transfer of electronhole pairs, and surface reaction, the holes $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+})$ on the valence band (VB) are able to oxidize water molecules to generate oxygen; in contrast, the electrons on the conduction band (CB) can reduce the protons to $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ [3]. Notably, previous works have illustrated that photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution mainly depends on the reduction ability of electrons in CB, demonstrating that the potential of the CB bottom should be taken into account [4]. Besides, both VB and CB regulation can generate free radicals to promote the degradation of pollutants. Furthermore, enhancing the reduction ability of electrons by optimizing CB would expedite the photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution and pollutants degradation of semiconductor materials. For instance, substituting Nb at Ta sites in ${\sf A g T a O}_{3}$ was able to control the CB bottom potential to promote the water splitting reaction [5], and incorporating functional linkers could efficiently manufacture a shift of the VB to higher energies in metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [6]. Therefore, the regulation of CB can introduce the possibility of facilitating photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution and pollutants degradation based on semiconductor materials.
In recent decades, numerous semiconductor materials have been investigated for photocatalysis, including metal oxides [7], metal sulfides [8], graphitic carbon nitride [9–11], covalent organic frameworks [12], and MOFs [13,14]. Among these materials, researchers recently achieved spin polarization by introducing defects into metal oxides and covalent organic frameworks, which ultimately enhanced photocatalysis [15,16]. Compared with metal oxides, defects are more accessible to be introduced in MOFs, which, constructed by metal clusters/ions and organic linkers and combined with abundant adjustability in structural and flexibility properties, can provide a tunable coordination microenvironment for efficient adsorption and photocatalysis [17–23]. Defect engineering has been adopted to manipulate the defect shape, pore size and electronic structure, including d-band center, charge distribution and spin moment in MOFs [22,24–27], which is a superior strategy to tune the electronic structure in MOFs further to promote photocatalytic performance [28,29]. Therefore, defect engineering for MOF synthesis presents multiple advantages for precisely manipulating defects [30]. To manipulate the density of defects, the thermal treatment of initial mixed-linker MOFs represents an essential method of removing partial linkers depending on the different melting points of the mixed linkers [31]. Despite the burgeoning interest in defect engineering and the improvement of defective MOFs, the development of monolithic and electronic structures for these flexible materials has not been thoroughly pursued. Consequently, it is expedient to investigate the variation of electronic structures that most probably affect the photocatalytic capability in defect-engineered MOFs.
