## 3.1. Defect engineering in MIL 25- $.m\%$
To readily control the density of defects, the ratios of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C-}$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ were set as $0\%,1\ \%,2\ \%,$ $5\%$ , $10\%$ and $20\%$ . TGA was adopted to confirm the construction of mixed-linker Ti-based MOFs. Fig. S1 shows a plateau from $200\ {^\circ}\mathrm{C}{-}324\ {^\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , illustrating that ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ was removed at temperatures higher than $324\mathrm{~\textdegreeC}$ . The Dweight $(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{wt}})$ was displayed to distinctly reveal the trend of weight variation (Fig. S2). Once the temperature rose to $324^{\circ}\mathsf C$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL$125\mathrm{-}10\ \%$ and $N H_{2}{-}\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}20\ \%$ incurred significant weight loss, and weight loss occurred at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $N H_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{-}1\mathrm{\%}$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL- $125-2\ \%$ , and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL- $125-5\ \%$ , which was attributed to the low proportion of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ . According to $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{wt}},$ it could be concluded that MIL- $125\mathrm{-}20\%$ contained the most ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ among the samples. To identify the specific proportion of defects, the dissolution $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra (Fig. S3) were utilized, and the proportions of the ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ linker could be calculated. As shown in Table S1, the real proportions of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ in $M\mathrm{IL}{-}125{-}0\ \%,$ MIL-125-1 %, MIL- $125-2\ \%$ , MIL- $125-5\ \%$ , MIL-125- $10\%$ and MIL$125\mathrm{-}20\ \%$ were $0\%2\%3\%9\%18\%$ and $32\%$ , respectively. It was obvious that the real proportions of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ were higher than the theoretical ratios in dosing, which was attributed to the electronegativity difference between $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ and $H_{2}B D C-N H_{2}$ , leading to competitive coordination between organic linkers [42]. As a result, it could be observed that a series of Ti-based MOFs containing different proportions of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ were synthesized successfully as we envisioned, and the missing linker defects were introduced into the MOFs after thermal treatment. SEM was employed to observe the effect of defects on the morphology of materials. As shown in Fig. 1a, b, and S4, the surface of MIL-125- $0\%$ was still relatively smooth. Meanwhile, with the increase in defects, the surfaces of MIL- $125-1\ \%$ , MIL- $125-2\ \%$ MIL- $125-5\ \%$ MIL-125- $10\%$ , and MIL- $125\mathrm{-}20\%$ became rough and even cracked. Hence, the surface topography of MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ was to a great extent affected by defects, and TEM was conducted to observe the influence of defects on the internal structure (Fig. 2c, d, S5). Comparing the images of the samples, the introduction of defects resulted in partial vacancies, which further expanded with the increase in defects and eventually formed obvious large-scale vacancies. Especially in MIL- $125-20\%$ , the structure was slightly damaged, presumably due to the preponderance of missing cluster defects caused by the high concentration of missing linker defects compared to MIL-125- $0\%$ , which could be confirmed by the element mapping images of MIL- $125{-}0\%$ and MIL- $125\mathrm{-}20\%$ (Fig. 1e, f). As displayed in Fig. S6-S10, the dark sites in the TEM image corresponded to the absence of Ti and O in the mapping images, illustrating that Ti loss in clusters was accompanied by O loss in linkers, which could be confirmed by the element contents listed in Table S2. In addition, the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms of the materials (Fig. S13) further illustrate the situation. Thus, the variations obtained from the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas were in the order of $\mathrm{MIL}-125{-}0\%>\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}2\%>$ $\mathrm{MIL-125–5~\%}>\mathrm{MIL-1}25–10~\%>\mathrm{MIL-1}25–20~\%$ (Table S3). The surface area of MIL- $125\mathrm{-}20\ \%$ was half that of MIL- $125{-}0\ \%$ because the intensive defects significantly decreased the surface area of MIL-125-20 %. Furthermore, the increased pore size of MIL-125- $5\%$ reflected that a mesoporous structure existed owing to the introduction of defects. On the whole, defects significantly affect the physical structures of the samples, which may alter the physical adsorption and mass transfer of the materials.
The following characterizations were carried out to investigate the chemical structures of the samples. The XRD (Fig. S14) patterns of MIL- $125\mathrm{-}0\%$ revealed that the significant characteristic peaks of
MIL-125 at $6.8^{\circ}$ and $16.8^{\circ}$ could be clearly observed, demonstrating the presence of partial MIL-125 [43]. New peaks at $25.7^{\circ}$ , $38^{\circ}$ , $48.7^{\circ}$ and $62.9^{\circ}$ were observed in MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ ${\mathrm{m}}=1,2,$ 5, 10, 20), which were assigned to the (10 1), (004), (20 0) and (204) lattice planes of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively, indicating that a small amount of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was formed by titanium oxide clusters after thermal treatment [44]. In addition, FTIR was employed to inspect the alteration of functional groups in the samples. In the FTIR (Fig. 2a) spectra of MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ , the bands at $1407~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1522~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be ascribed to the symmetric and asymmetric vibrations of the ${\mathsf{C O O}}^{-}$ compounded with the Ti centre [45,46], and the vibration at $3417~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be attributed to the stretching of the hydroxyl groups of the titanium oxide cluster formed in the decarboxylation process, illustrating that the linkers were successfully removed by pyrolysis in MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ (1, 2, 5, 10, 20) [47,48]. In addition, the bands representing the stretching vibration of O-Ti-O (Ti-oxo clusters) could be observed in the range of $400{\-}962{\ }\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [49]. Meanwhile, the vibration at $1627~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be attributed to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching [50]. Therefore, it could be inferred that the removal of linkers could introduce the vacancies of metal clusters in MIL$125\mathrm{-m}\%$ . The effect of thermal treatment was studied with XPS because the surface composition and chemical states of the asprepared MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ $\mathrm{\dot{m}}=2$ , 5, 10, 20) could reflect the structural changes produced by defects. As shown in Fig. S15, no apparent N element (approximately $400~\mathrm{eV}.$ ) existed on the surface of any test sample, illustrating that ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ was basically eliminated by thermal treatment. In the O 1s XPS spectrum (Fig. S16), two characteristic peaks at 529.7 and $531.4\mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to Ti-O and $c{-}0$ , respectively, which indicated the formation of the $_{\mathrm{T-O-C}}$ bond [51]. In addition, a chemical shift of the Ti-O bonding to a lower binding energy illustrated an increased local electron density (Fig. 2b). Meanwhile, the decline in the $c{-}0$ peak demonstrated that the linkers were successfully removed. As shown in Fig. S17, the peak of the C 1s spectrum could be divided into peaks at 284.6, 286.1, and 288.79 eV in MIL- $125\mathrm{-m}\%$ , which were assigned to ${\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{C}},{\mathsf{C}}{-}{\mathsf{C}}$ , and $c{-}0$ groups, respectively [52–54]. As conveyed from the amounts of chemical bonds in MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ derived from the C 1s data as well as listed in Table S4, the $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ and $c{-}0$ peaks both diminished as the ratios of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ were augmented, which indicated that the partial linkers were eliminated by thermal treatment. In the Ti 2p XPS spectrum (Fig. S18), MIL-125- $0\%$ showed two peaks at $458.78\mathrm{~\eV}$ and $464.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which corresponded to Ti $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ti $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ , indicating that titanium bonded to oxygen remained in oxidation state IV for the titanium oxide cluster [55]. The binding energies of these two peaks for MIL- $125-20\%$ shifted to $458.39\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $464.12\ \mathrm{eV}$ which might imply that missing cluster defects existed in the asprepared samples (Fig. 2c) [56,57]. In addition, the proportions of elements were calculated in Table S5 to verify the generation of missing cluster defects. The $\mathsf{C/T i}$ values in MIL- $125-2\%$ , MIL-125- $5\%$ , MIL-125- $10\%$ and MIL- $125\mathrm{-}20\ \%$ were 22.35, 24.13, 26.49 and 33.02, respectively, demonstrating the rise of Ti loss, that is, missing cluster defects, as the number of missing linker defects increased, which could be proven once more by the element mapping of TEM (Table S2). Above, FTIR and XPS both demonstrated the generation of missing cluster defects due to the aggregation of missing linker defects. EPR (Fig. 2d) was accomplished to further confirm the conformation of defects. MIL- $125\mathrm{-}0\%$ displayed a feeble signal located at $\mathrm{g}=2.003$ attributed to the O vacancies generated during the thermal treatment, while MIL- $125\mathrm{-}5\%$ exhibited an intense EPR signal [40]. In addition, more free electrons were detected after illumination in MIL- $125-5\ \%$ , illustrating that more electrons were excited from the VB to the CB. It could be inferred that unpaired electrons generated by O vacancies may redistribute around metal clusters that served as CB. However, the EPR signal of MIL- $125\mathrm{-}0\%$ did not fluctuate. In conclusion, mixed-linker Ti-based MOFs were successfully synthesized, and dramatically increased proportions of defects were introduced because partial linkers $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{D}{\mathsf{C}}-\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2})$ were removed. In particular, missing cluster defects were engendered since missing linker defects gathered, and the spatial structures of pristine MIL-125 and defective MIL-125 are shown in Fig. 2e.
Electrochemical tests were employed to examine the optoelectronic properties of all samples. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) diagram (Fig. 3a) displayed that the radius of all samples followed the order of MIL- $125{-}5\%<\mathsf{M I L}{-}125{-}2\ \%<\mathsf{M I L}{-}$ $125-0\%<\mathrm{MIL}-125-1\%<\mathrm{MIL}-125-10\%<\mathrm{MIL}-125-20\%$ %, illustrating that the moderate defects enhanced the charge transfer, which benefited from orbital overlap on account of the introduction of defects, while the charge transfer was suppressed owing to the destruction of the electron transfer channels since defects were excessive [58]. As shown in Fig. S19, the periodic on/off photocurrent response observed under visible light irradiation revealed that the intensity of the photocurrent followed the order of MIL-125- $5~{\mathcal{Z}}~{\mathrm{>~MIL-125-2~}}~{\mathcal{Z}}~{\mathrm{>~MIL-125-0~}}~{\mathcal{Z}}~{\mathrm{>~MIL-125-1~}}~{\mathcal{Z}}~{\mathrm{>~MIL-125-1~}}~{\mathcal{Z}}~{\mathrm{>~MIL-125-10~}}~{\mathcal{Z}}~{\mathrm{>~MIL-125-1~}}~{\mathcal{Z}}~{\mathrm{>~MIL-1~}}~$ $>\mathrm{MIL}-125{-20\ \%}$ , corresponding to the EIS results. The UV–vis diffuse reflection spectra (Fig. S20) of all MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ exhibited similar curves ranging from 200 to $800\mathrm{nm}$ , which originated from the close bandgap of approximately $3.12\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 3b), revealing that the energy of electronic transition was not changed by defects. It is proverbially acknowledged that the electrons could transfer from the aromatic amines to the titanium oxide clusters through conjugated $\pi$ electron transition in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125, which would narrow the bandgap [37]. Thus, the close bandgaps confirm the removal of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ . In addition, pure MIL-125- $0\%$ was white, while MIL-125-1 $\%$ , MIL- $125-2\%$ , MIL- $125-5\%$ MIL-125-10 %, and MIL- $125\mathrm{-}20\ \%$ were yellow before thermal treatment, and the colour became more obvious as the percentage of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C-}$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ increased, as shown in Fig. S21. However, the colours of all MIL- $125\mathrm{-m}\%$ became grey after thermal treatment, which also corresponded to the removal of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ . To further explore the influences of defects on the CB position of MIL- $125\mathrm{-m}\%$ , MottSchottky curves of MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ were obtained (Fig. 3d). According to the curves and simulation lines, the CB potentials of MIL- $125\mathrm{-m}$ $\%$ could be estimated to be $-0.28\mathrm{eV}$ (MIL-125-0 %), $-0.31$ eV (MIL125-1 %), $-0.35$ eV (MIL-125-2 $\%$ ), $-1.82$ eV (MIL-125-5 %), $-1.76\mathrm{\eV\(MIL-}125\mathrm{-}10\mathrm{\}\%\mathrm{)}$ and $-0.94\ \mathrm{eV}$ (MIL- $125\mathrm{-}20\ \%$ . In light of the obtained band gaps and CB positions, the valence bands of MIL- $125{-}0\ \%$ , MIL- $125-1\ \%$ , MIL- $125-2\ \%.$ , MIL- $125-5\ \%$ , MIL-125- $10\%$ and MIL- $125\text{\textperthousand}$ were also calculated (Fig. S22). In MIL125, the VB was composed of aromatic $\textsf{C}2\mathsf{p}$ orbitals in organic linkers, and the CB was composed of Ti 3d orbitals and O 2p orbitals in metal clusters [37,38]. Consequently, the missing linker defects and missing cluster defects engendered due to the missing linker defects are concentrated, as shown in FTIR and XPS [59], and would bring about the charge redistribution of the band structure. To investigate the variation in the band structure, three models were constructed according to pristine MIL-125 to evaluate the effects of missing linkers and cluster defects by DFT calculations [60]. As shown in Fig. S23, the models represent the defect-free MIL-125, MIL-125 with missing linker defects (ML-MIL-125), and MIL-125 with missing cluster defects (MC-MIL-125). The charge densities of CBM and VBM were favourably separated at the opposite sides of three configurations, and accumulated charges particularly increased in CBM. Furthermore, the defects moderately localized the CBM charge densities in ML-MIL-125 and MC-MIL125, and the charge distribution of the VBM was mainly concentrated on the other side (Fig. 3e, f, g), which heightened the electrostatic potential of the defective part, constructing a built-in electric field and facilitating electron-hole pair separation [61]. The corresponding gaps between the CBM and VBM for MIL-125, ML-MIL125 and MC-MIL-125 were calculated to be 1.48, 0.952 and $0.513\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. As a result, the elevation of the CB position seemed to correspond to missing linkers and cluster defects. Specifically, there were no missing linker defects in MIL- $125\mathrm{-}0\mathrm{~\%~}$ because of the thermal stability of $\mathsf{H}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{B D C}$ . As the ratio of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ increased, the missing cluster defects emerged from the concentration of missing linker defects in MIL- $125-1\ \%$ MIL$125-2\ \%$ and MIL- $125-5\ \%$ , resulting in a high charge density at defective sites, and the position of CB increased simultaneously. Subsequently, the density of missing cluster defects became dense, leading to structural damage, which further brought about the decline in the CB position. Hence, the flexibility of the CB position could be realized by the density of missing linker defects.
## 3.2. CB flexibility effects on photocatalytic performance
To explore the effect of CB position on photocatalytic ability, the photocatalytic activities of all MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ were evaluated in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation and pollutant degradation under simulated sunlight.
## 3.3. The relationship between defects and the electron spin state
To explore the effects of missing linkers and cluster defects on the charge distribution, DFT calculations of the metal cluster charge distribution in MIL-125, ML-MIL-125 and MC-MIL-125 were carried out. When missing linkers and cluster defects occurred, the charge distribution became uneven, as shown in Fig. 6a, b, and c. As a result of electron transfer, the unpaired electrons around defect sites tended to appear around adjacent titanium oxide groups, endowing the metal clusters that served as the CB with a high electron density, which was consistent with the chemical shifts in the O 1s and Ti 2p XPS spectra (Fig. 3b, c).
Given that both missing linkers and cluster defects resulted in a dense charge distribution, the spin states were further investigated to scrutinize the effects of defects. Thus, the spin polarization properties of MIL-125, ML-MIL-125 and MC-MIL-125 were also calculated. As mentioned above, the unpaired electrons induced by defects tended to be redistributed around metal clusters. The free electrons were affected by the electric field generated by the VB and the CB, and the spin direction changed due to spin-orbital coupling and hyperfine interactions. Thus, the spin moments $\left(\upmu_{\mathrm{{B}}}\right)$ of MIL-125, ML-MIL-125 and MC-MIL-125 were calculated as shown in Fig. S33. The total $\upmu_{\mathrm{B}}$ rose once defects were introduced, which may tend to provoke the changes in the electronic spin states. To clearly obtain the distribution of spin states, the 3D spatial distributions of spin polarization were calculated. As shown in Fig. 6d, e, and f, there was no apparent spin polarization for the defective free MIL-125. However, ML-MIL-125 and MC-MIL-125 exhibited increasing areas of spin-down spatial spin polarizations, which indicated the high difficulty of spin polarization reversal in configurations of defective materials. Remarkably, electrons substantially accumulated at the defect sites after the introduction of defects, which may result in spin polarization due to the interaction forces between electrons. The above results verify the existence of missing linker-dependent spin polarization, and electron spin polarization may further increase the energetic level of free charges, that is, the realization of CB position flexibility, and promote the efficiency of surface reactions for efficient photocatalysis.
## 3.4. Realization of CB flexibility by spin polarization in MIL-125- $.m\%$
Defective MIL- $125\mathrm{-m\%}$ constructed from Ti–O clusters and organic linkers exhibited astonishing photocatalytic performance: the VB was composed of organic linker $\mathtt{C2p}$ orbitals, and CB was composed of metal cluster $\textsf{O}2\mathsf{p}$ and Ti 3d orbitals. While a few defects were introduced, such as MIL- $125-1\ \%$ , MIL- $125-2\ \%$ , and MIL- $125-5\ \%$ , the effect of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production was upgraded with the increase in missing linkers and cluster defects, which contributed to the moderate defects that led to the redistribution of free charges. Meanwhile, the position of the CB showed an upwards trend. Subsequently, the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production decreased as the ratio of precursor ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}-{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ increased, since the missing cluster defects were concentrated, resulting in structural collapse in MIL-125- $10\%$ and MIL- $125-20\%$ . In the meantime, the potential of the CB started to become more positive. Therefore, the defects must have affected the band structure and charge states of the materials.
